# ACME certificate authorities. Copy to acme.env (gitignored) and uncomment one
# block, or several - each is an independent resolver with its own account and
# certificates. Then set TRAEFIK_CERT_RESOLVER in .env to the name you want as the
# instance default; a public web can pick a different one in the GUI.
#
# Naming: TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_<NAME>_ACME_*, where <NAME> is yours to
# choose. It is the name TRAEFIK_CERT_RESOLVER and the GUI refer to.
#
# Read once at startup: recreate Traefik after changing anything here
# (cd docker && docker compose up -d traefik).
#
# To change CA, add a NEW resolver rather than editing an existing one. An ACME
# account is bound to the CA that registered it, so repointing a resolver at a
# different CA leaves Traefik using an account that CA has never seen. A new name
# gets a new account, leaves the old certificates in place, and is reversible by
# putting TRAEFIK_CERT_RESOLVER back.

# --- Let's Encrypt -----------------------------------------------------------
# No External Account Binding. Free, 90-day certificates, and it validates any
# domain that passes the challenge - no pre-authorisation.
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_LETSENCRYPT_ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_LETSENCRYPT_ACME_STORAGE=/letsencrypt/acme.json
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_LETSENCRYPT_ACME_HTTPCHALLENGE_ENTRYPOINT=web
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_LETSENCRYPT_ACME_KEYTYPE=EC384
# Staging while testing - untrusted certificates, but forgiving rate limits.
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_LETSENCRYPT_ACME_CASERVER=https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory

# --- ZeroSSL -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Requires EAB credentials from your ZeroSSL account.
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_STORAGE=/letsencrypt/acme.json
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_HTTPCHALLENGE_ENTRYPOINT=web
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_KEYTYPE=EC384
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_CASERVER=https://acme.zerossl.com/v2/DV90
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_EAB_KID=
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_ZEROSSL_ACME_EAB_HMACENCODED=

# --- Any other CA ------------------------------------------------------------
# Set the CA's own directory URL, and its EAB credentials if it issues them.
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_STORAGE=/letsencrypt/acme.json
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_HTTPCHALLENGE_ENTRYPOINT=web
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_KEYTYPE=EC384
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_CASERVER=https://acme.example.org/acme/directory
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_EAB_KID=
# TRAEFIK_CERTIFICATESRESOLVERS_CUSTOM_ACME_EAB_HMACENCODED=
#
# Commercial CAs often expect the domain to be authorised on the account first.
# Such a CA rejects the order outright rather than failing the challenge - the
# Traefik log shows the CA's own message, e.g. "ACME entry not found".

# Key types: EC256, EC384, RSA2048, RSA4096, RSA8192.
#
# Changes here take effect at the NEXT issuance. Traefik does not re-issue a
# hostname that already has a valid certificate - not for a new key type, and not
# for a switch to a different resolver; it logs "No ACME certificate generation
# required for domains" and keeps serving what it has. The certificate panel
# (Configuration -> Application Settings -> Routing & TLS) shows the renewal date,
# which is when a change actually lands. To force it sooner, remove that domain's
# entry from acme.json in the acme_storage volume and recreate Traefik.
